          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Electrochemical cells are two half-cells that separate oxidation and reduction reactions, allowing current to flow through an external wire. The anode, where oxidation occurs, connects to the cathode, where reduction takes place. A salt bridge completes the circuit by allowing ions to flow, maintaining electrical neutrality.Cell notation provides a standardized way to describe these cells, with reactions at the anode on the left and at the cathode on the right."
              }
            ],
            "id": "2040472587",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Standard reduction potentials help predict the cell potential, reflecting a redox process's driving force. This potential is linked to free energy change and equilibrium constants, making it essential for designing efficient energy storage and conversion systems."
              }
            ],
            "id": "r4ohof2g9v9hg4d",
            "type": "p"
          },
          {
            "children": [
              {
                "text": "Understanding electrochemical cells and their potentials is crucial for many applications, including sustainable energy solutions and industrial electroplating processes."
              }
            ],
            "id": "1h2tg67ccdlqbi9",
            "type": "p"
          }
